            pw_intro: `A part-worth measures how much a level adds to preference against the base level (60 km, hub motor, built-in battery, no warranty or GPS). Only differences can be interpreted. Price is utility per €100.`,
            pw_title: `Part-worths by segment (95% interval)`,
            r1_assumed_1: `What buyers value and how much they would pay for it: that is what the study is for, and it will measure it with error.`,
            r1_assumed_2: `How many shoppers will know and find the Hiri in its first season: a conjoint simulator assumes all of them do.`,
            r1_assumed_3: `How Tornell will react.`,
            r1_attrs_title: `Which attributes go into the study`,
            r1_brief: `You have eight weeks until the March report and up to €135,000 of research budget. Three decisions: the method, the attributes that go into the study and the sample. The tables say what each option measures, what it cannot measure and its known biases. None is free and none sees everything: whatever the study does not measure, you will have to assume.`,
            r1_debate: `Is it worth €30,000 more for incentive-aligned data when the hypothetical bias is known and you could correct it with judgment? Who decides which attributes are worth measuring: engineering, which knows what it can change, or the customer, who knows what matters? Would you rather have 300 well-chosen people or 1,500 who are easy to reach?`,
            r1_known_1: `The market: about 40,000 people a year shop for a mid-range urban e-bike at independent dealers; 65% to commute and 35% enthusiasts (industry association).`,
            r1_known_2: `The rival: Tornell sold about 14,000 bicycles in 2027 with 90 km, a hub motor and 22 kg at €2,290.`,
