            r2_title: `Round 2 · Monday 6 March 2028: design, price and launch run`,
            r2_unmeasured: `Not measured, so their value is an assumption: %A%.`,
            r3_assumed_1: `Whether Tornell will cut again or launch a new model.`,
            r3_assumed_3: `How much a second version takes from the first. The simulator is a logit: when two versions are very similar, it tends to give them share that in reality they take from each other.`,
            r3_brief: `On Friday 1 September Tornell cut its bicycle from €2,290 to €1,990. From September you build to order. The answer holds until December 2029: match (€300 off); keep the price and add a 3-year battery warranty (€55 a bicycle in expected claims); keep the price and spend €400,000 on advertising and dealer support (€100,000 this autumn and €300,000 in 2029, so that more shoppers know the Hiri: +4 points of reach this autumn and +8 in 2029); or keep the price and do nothing else. Second version for 2029: none; Hiri Pro (120 km, central motor, 22 kg with a carbon frame, €3,190, costs €1,300, €350,000 of tooling); Hiri Urban (90 km, hub, removable battery, 19.6 kg, €2,190, costs €920, €250,000); or Hiri Go (60 km, hub, 18.5 kg, €1,690, costs €760, €200,000).`,
            r3_debate: `Matching protects share, and with it shelf space in 2029; holding protects margin. What do your buyers value more: €300 or a three-year warranty? If you never measured the warranty, what are you assuming? And a second version: new buyers, or the same buyers at a different margin?`,
